==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|arreinen}}, from {{der|en|fro|araisnier||to address, to verify}} (whence modern {{cog|fr|arraisonner||to verify cargo, to arraign}}), from {{m|fro|raison||reason}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈɹeɪn/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-arraign.wav|Audio (RP)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪn|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To officially charge someone in a [[court of law]].
#: {{ux|en|He was '''arraigned''' in Washington, D.C., on the 25th of that month on charges of treason.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ben Protess|title=Trump Is Indicted, Becoming First Ex-President to Face Criminal Charges|work=The New York Times|date=2023-03-30|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/03/30/nyregion/trump-indictment-hush-money-charges.html|issn=0362-4331|author2=Jonah E. Bromwich|author3=William K. Rashbaum|author4=Kate Christobek|author5=Nate Schweber|passage=He will then be '''arraigned''', at which point the specific charges will be unsealed.}}
# To [[call to account]], or accuse, before the [[bar]] of reason, taste, or any other [[tribunal]].
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis}}
#*: They will not dare to '''arraign''' you for want of knowledge.
#* {{RQ:Isaac Taylor Saturday Evening|passage=It is not arrogance, but timidity, of which the Christian body should now be '''arraigned''' by the world.}}

